## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle on API testing: idempotent long-running operations and eventual consistency for per-tenant exports, a common real-world pattern.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency-Key handling across retries
- Long-running jobs with 202 acceptance and polling
- Status lifecycle: PENDING -> RUNNING -> FINAL (COMPLETED/FAILED)
- Worker downtime simulation and resilience
- Per-tenant data isolation in test data setup

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure idempotency and status polling
async function testExportIdempotent(key){
  const r1 = await fetch('/exports', {method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify({format:'csv'})});
  const j1 = await r1.json();
  const r2 = await fetch('/exports', {method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify({format:'csv'})});
  const j2 = await r2.json();
  console.assert(j1.job_id === j2.job_id, 'Idempotent key should return same job_id');
  let status = 'PENDING';
  const job = j1.job_id;
  while(['PENDING','RUNNING'].includes(status)){
    const s = await fetch(`/exports/${job}`); const js = await s.json(); status = js.status;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate worker downtime and verify fallback behavior?
- What metrics and logs would you collect to confirm eventual consistency across multiple clients?

